  'draft', 'foundation',
  $x$A 34-year-old woman presents to her GP with a 7-month history of persistent worry about her job, finances, and her children's health, present most days and difficult to control. She reports muscle tension, poor sleep, feeling constantly on edge, and difficulty concentrating at work. She has no significant past medical history and does not use alcohol or recreational drugs. Physical examination and thyroid function tests are normal.$x$,
  'What is the most appropriate first-line pharmacological treatment if psychological therapy alone is insufficient?',
  array['GAD','generalised anxiety disorder','SSRI']
),
('e3000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002', 'ukmla', '8d54a4e2-b8b2-4f45-a133-a7652815561e',
  'draft', 'foundation',
  'A 28-year-old man attends the emergency department reporting a 15-minute episode of sudden intense fear, chest tightness, palpitations, sweating, trembling, and a feeling of impending doom while on the underground train. He felt he was going to die. This is his fourth similar episode in 2 months, and he has since started avoiding public transport. Examination, ECG, and troponin are all normal.',
  'What is the most likely diagnosis?',
  array['panic disorder','panic attack']
),
